  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 324 AM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\n     TODAY THROUGH THURSDAY\n\nEarly this morning, a band of mid-level WAA within an area of \nmodest elevated instability is aiding in producing and \nmaintaining scattered showers and storms extending from northern\nMissouri, across central Kansas, and into the Oklahoma \nPanhandle. Most of this activity is likely to remain sub-severe \nwith localized flooding and semi-frequent cloud-to-ground \nlightning being the primary concerns. As this axis of WAA lifts \nnorthward throughout the morning hours, this activity will lift \nnorthward with it ending rain chances by early this afternoon. \nWith rain and clouds clearing much of the area (especially \nsouth of I-70), afternoon temperatures will surge back into the \n90s across southern and southeast Kansas. This warm up is \nprimarily being supported by mid/upper ridging building westward\nfrom the southeastern CONUS. Across far southeast Kansas, \nafternoon highs may reach 100. With dewpoints around 70, heat \nindices are likely to exceed 105. The ridge will continue to \nstrengthen and expand its influence across the central plains on\nThursday. This will lead to another afternoon with highs in the\nmid to upper 90s. Currently, it appears the airmass will be \nslightly drier, but heat indices will likely reach or exceed 100\nacross much of the area.\n\n     FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY\n\nMid-range guidance continues to show that the mid/upper ridge \nresponsible for the mid-week heat will weaken rather quickly as it \nget squeezed between a retrograding upper low over the Gulf \ncoastline and the region's next system arriving from out over \nthe western CONUS. Still, it won't weaken enough to prevent \nanother couple of very warm days across the region with highs in\nthe mid to upper 90s. At this time, a cold front is forecast to\napproach the area by Friday night, and will slowly lumber its \nway across the area throughout the weekend. Global models have \nbeen trending slower with the arrival of the cold front, now \nhaving it push through the forecast area late Saturday night. \nHowever, global ensembles still seem to be on board with more \nfall-like air arriving either Sunday or Monday. In addition to \nthe potential for cooler temperatures, periodic chances for \nshowers and storms remain in the forecast from late Friday night\nthrough the weekend as the front slowly pushes southward across\nKansas.",
